[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue: the parser reads dates month first. That
is true for most of its reports. It is not true for all of them. The reports hold three
problems, and each has its own ticket in the
[tickets repo](https://github.com/valychbreak/kb-4471/issues).

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Fixing the parser closes the first problem only.
</Callout>

## What is in it

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table is computed from the report export, by the tenant's locale and booking form
(`causes.ts`). It does not use intake's tag, which is only a guess from the reporter's browser.

| problem | what happens | ticket |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | The shared parser reads every date month first. Day-first tenants get flipped dates. | [issue 1](https://github.com/valychbreak/kb-4471/issues/1) |
| `hint` | booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. People in day-first locales type it that way and the form reads it day first. Only form reports. | [issue 2](https://github.com/valychbreak/kb-4471/issues/2) |
| one-day move | The date shifts by one day, not a month and day. The desk read these reports that way (`desk-notes.json`). It looks like a timezone or DST bug. | [issue 3](https://github.com/valychbreak/kb-4471/issues/3) |

The one-day reports sit inside the `parser` row of the table, as the footnote under it says.
The count for the `parser` ticket leaves them out, because they are a different problem.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Why it was merged as one

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line. During this
incident that merged reports that share a subject but not a cause, and kept apart reports that
share a cause but not a subject. See [triage](/kb/triage/dedupe) for what to match on instead:
the tenant's locale and form.

## Lessons

- A big ticket can be several bugs reported many times. Count by cause, not by subject.
- A tag guessed at intake is not a cause. Check it against the tenant.

